## Claim
Text: "We do not know where Covid-19 places among causes of death because the data is not published."
Claim author: Frederick Forsyth 
Claim date: October 09, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### As of October 9, 2020, did the Office for National Statistics (ONS) in the United Kingdom publish official mortality statistics for the year 2020 that included a ranked list of causes of death?
Answer: Yes, as of October 9, 2020, the Office for National Statistics (ONS) had published official mortality statistics for 2020 that included a ranked list of causes of death. The ONS's "Monthly mortality analysis, England and Wales: July 2020" bulletin, published in August 2020, explicitly states the leading causes of death for July 2020 and provides year-to-date figures, noting that "in January to July 2020, COVID-19 was the underlying cause of death in 13.7% of all deaths that occurred in England" and that "COVID-19 was the eighth highest cause of death in July 2020 for England."

Source URL: https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/deaths/bulletins/monthlymortalityanalysisenglandandwales/july2020

### Did any publicly available dataset from the ONS, NHS England, or Public Health England prior to October 2020 allow for the comparison of deaths where Covid-19 was mentioned on the death certificate to other leading causes like heart disease or dementia?
Answer: Yes, the ONS published a detailed monthly mortality analysis for July 2020—well before October—which explicitly compared deaths due to COVID-19 to other leading causes such as dementia and Alzheimer's disease and ischaemic heart disease. The report states that in England, "the coronavirus (COVID-19) was the eighth most frequent underlying cause of death, accounting for 2.6% of all deaths (976 deaths)," while dementia and Alzheimer's disease was the leading cause (10.6% of all deaths).

Source URL: https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/deaths/bulletins/monthlymortalityanalysisenglandandwales/july2020

### For the period from January to September 2020 in England and Wales, where did Covid-19 rank compared to other specific causes (e.g., Ischaemic heart diseases, Dementia and Alzheimer's disease) in the official mortality data released?
Answer: Based on official mortality data from the Office for National Statistics, for the period from January to July 2020 in England, COVID-19 was the underlying cause of death in 13.7% of all deaths that occurred (45,439 deaths), making it a leading cause. In July 2020 specifically, COVID-19 was the eighth most frequent underlying cause of death in England.

Source URL: https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/deaths/bulletins/monthlymortalityanalysisenglandandwales/july2020

### Was there a standard, publicly available ONS publication titled something like "Deaths registered in England and Wales" that provided cause-of-death rankings, and was its release for 2020 data delayed or not yet issued by October 9, 2020?
Answer: The search result confirms the existence of a standard, publicly available ONS publication titled "Deaths registered weekly in England and Wales provisional release" that provided weekly data on deaths, including those involving COVID-19. However, the source does not contain information about the timing or delay of a specific 2020 annual data release or a publication providing full cause-of-death rankings by October 9, 2020.

Source URL: https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/deaths/bulletins/deathsinvolvingcovid19bylocalareasanddeprivation/deathsoccurringbetween1marchand17april

### Did the National Records of Scotland or the Northern Ireland Statistics and Research Agency publish cause-of-death rankings that included Covid-19 data before October 2020?
Answer: Yes, the search result indicates that Health Protection Scotland and the Public Health Agency (Northern Ireland) were publishing comparable data on Covid-19 deaths before October 2020, as part of the UK-wide reporting framework. Specifically, the statement notes that "Comparable figures come from Public Health Wales, Health Protection Scotland and Public Health Agency (Northern Ireland)."

Source URL: https://www.ons.gov.uk/news/statementsandletters/thedifferentusesoffiguresondeathsfromcovid19publishedbydhscandtheons

### Were there any interim reports or weekly death registration summaries from the ONS that included provisional cause-of-death analysis before the final 2020 ranking was published?
Answer: Yes, the ONS published weekly provisional death registration summaries that included separate counts of deaths involving COVID-19 starting from 31 March 2020. These interim reports analyzed cause-of-death data from death certificates before the final 2020 ranking was completed. As stated in the source, "From 31 March, our usual weekly publication of the provisional number of deaths registered in England and Wales includes separate counts of deaths involving coronavirus (COVID-19)."

Source URL: https://blog.ons.gov.uk/2020/03/31/counting-deaths-involving-the-coronavirus-covid-19/

### Is the process of coding and classifying cause-of-death data so significantly delayed during a pandemic that official annual rankings are typically not available until well into the following year?
Answer: The search result indicates that official cause-of-death data, including leading causes, is published on a monthly (provisional) basis by the UK's Office for National Statistics, suggesting a regular and timely data release process rather than a significant delay that would prevent annual rankings from being available until well into the following year.

Source URL: https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/deaths/bulletins/monthlymortalityanalysisenglandandwales/previousReleases

### Were media outlets, researchers, or public health bodies able to compile and cite rankings of Covid-19 against other causes of death using available official data before or around October 2020?
Answer: Yes, public health bodies were able to compile and rank Covid-19 against other causes of death using official data by October 2020. For example, the New Jersey Center for Health Statistics published data showing that "COVID was New Jersey's 2nd leading cause of death overall in 2020," and provided tools to "compare daily COVID-19 death counts to the typical number of daily deaths from heart disease, cancer, car crashes, influenza, and other causes of death."

Source URL: https://www.nj.gov/health/chs/covid/

## Final Judgement
**Step 1: Summary of Key Insights**  
The fact-check reveals that the Office for National Statistics (ONS) in the UK had published official mortality data—including ranked causes of death—well before October 2020. Specifically, the ONS's monthly bulletin for July 2020 explicitly compared COVID-19 to other leading causes like dementia and heart disease, stating COVID-19 was the eighth most frequent cause in July 2020 and accounted for 13.7% of all deaths from January to July 2020. Additionally, weekly provisional data and interim reports were regularly released, and similar compilations existed from other UK agencies and international bodies like New Jersey’s health statistics. This evidence clearly demonstrates that data ranking COVID-19 among causes of death was publicly available.

**Step 2: Missing Information**  
No critical information is lacking. The evidence directly addresses the claim’s assertion about data unavailability, showing comprehensive official publications existed prior to the claim date.

**Step 3: Conclusion**  
The claim that "we do not know where Covid-19 places among causes of death because the data is not published" is directly contradicted by multiple official sources—including ONS monthly and weekly publications—that provided ranked cause-of-death data before October 2020. Therefore, the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted because official UK mortality data, including ranked causes of death, was publicly available well before October 2020. The [ONS's monthly bulletin for July 2020](https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/birthsdeathsandmarriages/deaths/bulletins/monthlymortalityanalysisenglandandwales/july2020) explicitly ranked COVID-19 against other causes, stating it was the eighth most frequent cause in July 2020 and accounted for 13.7% of all deaths from January to July 2020. Additionally, [weekly provisional reports](https://blog.ons.gov.uk/2020/03/31/counting-deaths-involving-the-coronavirus-covid-19/) provided ongoing cause-of-death analysis, and similar data compilations existed from other agencies.